<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Slovakia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Slovakia is a landlocked country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czechia</a>. From the 11th century until 1918 its lands formed the northern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, and they then became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>. Apart from a German client state of 1939–1945, it has been independent only since the peaceful dissolution of Czechoslovakia on 1 January 1993, and it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 2004.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slovakia.jpg" alt="Slovakia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Ladislav Čisárik and Ladislav Vrtel Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Slovakia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tens of thousands of graves excavated in Hungary, <b>Slovakia</b>, Austria and Serbia document the culture of this period, especially the cast bronze belt fittings decorated with griffins and tendril patterns that distinguish the Late Avar era.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Frontiers were fixed only after fighting: troops occupied the German-speaking borderlands of Bohemia and Moravia, defended <b>Slovakia</b> against the Hungarian Soviet Republic in 1919 and clashed with Poland over Teschen, which was divided in 1920.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Great Moravia was the first major state that was predominantly West Slavic, centred on the Morava River, which gave it its name, in today's eastern Czech Republic and western <b>Slovakia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Basic treaties with Romania, <b>Slovakia</b> and Ukraine renounced territorial claims, though the rights of the roughly five million Hungarians living abroad, mostly in Romania, Slovakia and Serbia, still cause tensions; relations with Serbia grew close, while those with Ukraine deteriorated from 2017 over the Hungarian minority there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The army helped the border guards against illegal crossings from the Eastern bloc frontier until Hungary and <b>Slovakia</b> joined Schengen in 2008.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-austria">Republic of Austria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It is generally held to have been standardised by two Byzantine missionaries from Thessaloniki, Cyril and his brother Methodius, for a mission to Great Moravia, roughly today’s eastern Czech Republic and western <b>Slovakia</b>, sent in answer to a request by its ruler, Duke Rastislav, for a Slavonic liturgy.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-church-slavonic">Old Church Slavonic · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
